EXT. DEAD DRIVE-IN THEATER - NIGHT

A cracked lot. A blank screen the size of a barn. Speaker poles stand in rows like headstones. One lamp burns over a concession shack.

PELL PELLEGRINO, 50s, in a loud tie and a camel coat, sits on the hood of a Buick with a briefcase on his knee. ODESSA LANE, 40, a plainclothes detective in a gray raincoat, walks across the gravel with her hands visible.

ODESSA
You're early.

PELL
I like to see who comes.

ODESSA
Just me.

PELL
Just you. Nice of them.

ODESSA
You asked for me.

PELL
I asked for the one who wouldn't bring a van.

Odessa stops ten feet away. She lowers her hands, one of them drifting to the lining of her coat. Pell opens the briefcase an inch and looks inside, then up. A moth circles the lamp, crashing, crashing. Down the lot, a dog barks twice and gives up. Pell slaps the case shut, hops off the hood, and takes a step, and Odessa takes a step, and the gravel cracks under both of them.

PELL (CONT'D)
You know what this lot used to show?

ODESSA
Westerns.

PELL
Every Friday. My father brought me. Dust, horses, a man in a hat who always knew the way out.

ODESSA
Pell.

PELL
He never did. Know the way. He just had the hat.

ODESSA
Where's Sal?

PELL
Sal's on the snack bar roof. With a rifle.

She doesn't look up. She keeps her eyes on his.

ODESSA
You're telling me.

PELL
I'm telling you because I like you.

ODESSA
That's not why.

PELL
No. I'm telling you because you'll understand it's an insult.

ODESSA
He won't shoot.

PELL
He would. But I'd like to make you an offer first.

ODESSA
Go on.

PELL
I'll give you the briefcase. All of it. Names, dates, drops, three judges, and the mayor's cousin. In return, tonight, you go home. You never found me.

ODESSA
That's a lot of names for a woman who can't be trusted.

PELL
You're the only one who can't be bought. That's the point.

She looks at the roof of the snack bar. A silhouette, a barrel. She looks back.

ODESSA
Put it on the ground.

PELL
Detective.

ODESSA
Put it on the ground, Mr. Pellegrino, and tell your friend to climb down.

Pell holds the briefcase for a long beat. Then he lowers it to the gravel. The lamp flickers. On the snack bar roof, the silhouette does not move.
